    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Let's denote the third side of the triangle as \\(x\\). Since the triangle has a right angle between the sides of lengths 6 cm and 8 cm, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to solve for \\(x\\).\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that \\(a^2 + b^2 = c^2\\) for a right triangle labeled using legs \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) and hypotenuse \\(c\\), where \\(c\\) is the longest side. In this case, we have:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6^2 = 8^2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 36 = 64\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\(36\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\nx^2 = 28\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(x\\), we take the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\nx = \\sqrt{28}\n\\]\n\nThus, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle to the nearest hundredth (approximately up to two decimal places) would be:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{28} \\approx 5.29\n\\]",
